.TH RDB 1A 4-Dec-91 \& \&
.SH NAME
rdb \- create and manage hard disk partition tables
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
rdb [-v] [-H] [-e] [-E] device
rdb -c [-s cylindersize] [-L n] -d disksize device [name start length]...
rdb -a device name start length
rdb -p partition [-b] [-B] [-m] [-M] [-C n] [-F n] [-P n] [-R n] device
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I rdb
has three major purposes:  to display a hard disk partition table, to
create a hard disk partition table, or to modify a hard disk partition table.
.PP
.I rdb
with no options or with the -H option displays the partition table
on the specified device, if there is one.
.PP
.I rdb
with the -c option creates a disk partition table on the specified device.
.I rdb
with the -a option adds a partition to the partition table.
.I rdb
with the -p option modifies parameters associated with the selected
partition.
.SH OPTIONS
.I rdb
recognizes the following options:
.TP
-a

Add a partition to an existing partition table.

Partition starting and length
parameters must be multiples of the cylinder size.  You must ensure that
partitions do not overlap each other, the initial reserved cylinders
or the end of the disk.

The first 100 blocks are reserved for the partition table and rigid
disk block.  If you expect to install AmigaDos on this disk, you must
reserve the first 2 cylinders.

The first partition starts at the first cylinder boundary above the
reserved blocks.

.TP
-b
Make the selected partition non-bootable.
.TP
-B
Make the selected partition bootable.
.TP
-c
Create a new partition table and write it to the specified device.
An optional partition list may follow the arguments.

WARNING: This option destroys all data on the device.

.TP
-C n
Set the number of custom boot blocks for the selected partition to n.
An Amiga Unix boot partition requires 2 custom boot blocks.
.TP
-d n
Set the capacity of the hard drive in blocks.
The capacity of the drive is rounded down to the nearest cylinder
boundary.  See the -s option.
.TP
-e
Clear the "last drive in system" bit on the specified device.
.TP
-E
Set the "last drive in system" bit on the specified device.
.TP
-F n
Set the file system type on the selected partition to n, where n is an
eight digit hex number.  Valid file system types are:
.PP
.nf
0x444f5300      DOS\\0        AmigaDOS old filesystem
0x444f5301      DOS\\1        AmigaDOS fast filesystem (ffs)
0x554e4900      UNI\\0        Unix boot partition
0x554e4901      UNI\\1        Unix s5 filesystem
0x554e4902      UNI\\2        Unix ufs filesystem
0x72657376      RESV         Reserved partition (Unix swap)
.fi

NOTE:  The 0x is required.
.TP
-H
Present partition information in a human-readable form.
.TP
-L n
Make rdb use block n as the default position for the partition table.
If not set, the default position is block 0.  The -L 2 option is
is required on systems that do not have Kickstart 2.0 or newer roms,
for compatibility with AmigaDOS.
.TP
-m
Make the selected partition not mountable.  Non-bootable UNIX partitions
require -m.
.TP
-M
Make the selected partition mountable.
Bootable UNIX partitions and all AmigaDOS partitions require -M.
.TP
-p n
Select partition n.  The b, B, C, P, f and F options operate on the
selected partition.  Partitions are numbered starting at 1.
.TP
-P n
Set the boot priority on the selected partition to n.
Boot priority 5 is reserved for the floppy drive.
The system
boots the bootable (-M -B) partition having the highest boot priority.
New AmigaUNIX
systems have priority 2 assigned to the UNIX_Boot partition and 0 or 1
to AmigaDOS.
.TP
-R n
Set the number of filesystem reserved blocks to n.  AmigaDOS partitions
require -R 2.
.TP
-s cylindersize
Set the blocks per cylinder to cylindersize.  Default blocks per
cylinder is 64. A cylinder size of at least 64 blocks is required if
AmigaDOS partitions are going to be added to the disk.
.TP
-v
Enables verbose output.
.PP
The value displayed by the
.I ddsize
command may be used as the disksize value.
.I rdb
requires that partitions begin and end on cylinder boundaries.

.SH EXAMPLES
Find out the actual size of the scsi id 6 hard drive using the command
.nf

        ddsize /dev/dsk/c6d0s0

.fi
If it displays 205074 for a particular drive, the command
.nf

        rdb -c -s 64 -L 2 -d 205074 /dev/dsk/c6d0s0

.fi
will initialize its partition table without creating partitions.

WARNING: The -c option will destroy all data on the disk.

Use the -a option later to add partitions.
The commands
.nf

        rdb -a /dev/dsk/c6d0s0 Home  128   81920
        rdb -a /dev/dsk/c6d0s0 Boot2 82048 4096

.fi
add a 40 megabyte partition and a 2 megabyte partition as the first
and second partitions on the disk at scsi id 6.  The start offset 128
of the first partition allows space for the two 64-block reserved
cylinders at the beginning of the disk.  Note that all start and
length offsets are a multiple of the cylinder size.  The command
.nf


        rdb -p 1 -m -F 0x554e4901 /dev/dsk/c6d0s0

.fi
Sets parameters for partition 1 on scsi disk 6 appropriate for a
non-bootable UNIX filesystem partition.
.nf


        rdb -p 2 -M -B -C 2 -P 2 -F 0x554e4900 /dev/dsk/c6d0s0

.fi
Sets parameters for partition 2, scsi id 6 appropriate for an
AmigaUNIX bootable partition: automount, bootable, 2 custom boot
blocks, priority 2, and filesystem type UNI\\0.

.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.I rdb's
exit status indicates success or failure.  An appropriate message is
written to standard error output upon failure.

.SH "SEE ALSO"
ddsize(1A), showrdb(1A)

.SH NOTES

To set up AmigaDOS partitions, leave unallocated space and use the
tools on the AmigaDOS Install floppy, Version 2.04 or later.

Using HDToolBox on a disk configured with less than 64
blocks-pre-cylinder is not a pleasant experience.  Don't try it, you
won't like it.

You must use -L 2 with -c if you do not have Kickstart 2.0 or later.

If you are using a 2090 or 2090A disk controller, you cannot install
AmigaUNIX and AmigaDOS on the same disk.
